'Americano':['aperitif','aperitivo highball','30 ml bitter aperitivo; 30 ml sweet vermouth; soda water','Build over ice in a rocks glass; top soda; orange or lemon.','Gentle bitterness, wine sweetness, lively bubbles.','First called Milano-Torino in some accounts, reflecting ingredient origins; “Americano” became associated with its popularity among American visitors.'],
'Rusty Nail':['whiskey','spirit-forward','45 ml Scotch whisky; 25 ml Drambuie','Build over a large ice cube; stir gently; lemon twist optional.','Honeyed herbs, malt, warm spice.','A Scotch-and-Drambuie combination that gained popularity in mid-20th-century cocktail culture; exact invention is unclear.'],
'Godfather':['whiskey','spirit-forward','45 ml Scotch whisky; 25 ml amaretto','Build over ice in a rocks glass; stir gently.','Nutty sweetness over malt and oak.','A 1970s-era two-ingredient drink. The name is often linked to the film, but the precise naming story is uncertain.'],
'Godmother':['vodka','spirit-forward','45 ml vodka; 25 ml amaretto','Build over ice in a rocks glass; stir gently.','Almond-like sweetness with a clean spirit base.','A simple variation on the Godfather; no single verified origin claim.'],
'Golden Dream':['vodka','sour','30 ml Galliano; 30 ml orange liqueur; 30 ml orange juice; 15 ml cream','Shake with ice; strain into chilled cocktail glass.','Creamy orange, vanilla and herbal sweetness.','A mid-20th-century cocktail later recognized by the IBA; attribution varies across sources.'],
'Sex on the Beach':['vodka','tropical','40 ml vodka; 20 ml peach schnapps; 40 ml orange juice; 40 ml cranberry juice','Shake with ice; strain over ice in a highball; orange garnish.','Peachy, fruity, sweet-tart.','A 1980s American cocktail with competing origin stories; it rose alongside the popularity of peach schnapps.'],
'Tequila Sunrise':['tequila','highball','45 ml blanco tequila; 90 ml orange juice; 15 ml grenadine','Build tequila and orange juice over ice; pour grenadine slowly; garnish orange.','Orange-forward, sweet, with agave beneath.','The modern layered version is associated with 1970s California; earlier Tequila Sunrise recipes were different.'],
'Long Island Iced Tea':['vodka','highball','15 ml each vodka, gin, white rum, tequila and orange liqueur; 25 ml lemon; 20 ml syrup; cola to top','Shake spirits, citrus and syrup briefly; strain over fresh ice; top cola.','Citrusy, sweet, cola-like; deceptively spirit-heavy.','Competing origin tales place versions in 1920s Tennessee or 1970s Long Island; no single claim is settled.'],
'Caipirinha':['cachaça','sour','60 ml cachaça; 1 lime; 2 tsp fine sugar','Muddle lime wedges gently with sugar; add cachaça and ice; stir.','Fresh lime oils, grassy cane, direct sweetness.','Brazil’s national cocktail, made with cachaça. Its exact origin is uncertain; early medicinal lime-and-spirit preparations are sometimes cited.'],
'Black Russian':['vodka','spirit-forward','50 ml vodka; 20 ml coffee liqueur','Build over ice in a rocks glass; stir gently.','Coffee sweetness and clean, warming spirit.','Often credited to bartender Gustave Tops at Brussels’ Hotel Metropole around 1949; the account is widely repeated, though archival detail is limited.'],
'Bramble':['gin','modern sour','50 ml gin; 25 ml lemon; 12.5 ml syrup; 15 ml crème de mûre float','Shake gin, lemon and syrup; strain over crushed ice; drizzle blackberry liqueur.','Bright lemon, crushed ice, ripe blackberry.','Created by Dick Bradsell in London in the 1980s, inspired by foraging for blackberries on the Isle of Wight.'],
'Boulevardier':['whiskey','bitter aperitivo','45 ml bourbon or rye; 30 ml bitter aperitivo; 30 ml sweet vermouth','Stir with ice; strain over a large cube or into a coupe; orange peel.','Rich, bittersweet, warming, oak and orange.','Published by Erskine Gwynne in Paris in 1927. A whiskey-led relative of the Negroni, though its exact earlier history is unclear.'],
'French 75':['gin','sparkling','30 ml gin; 15 ml lemon; 15 ml simple syrup; 60 ml Champagne','Shake first three; strain into flute; top with sparkling wine.','Bright, dry, lifted bubbles with botanical structure.','Named for the French 75 mm field gun, likely reflecting its lively effect. The drink appears in early 20th-century sources; recipes vary.'],
'Mojito':['rum','highball','50 ml white rum; 25 ml lime; 2 tsp sugar; mint; soda water','Gently press mint with sugar and lime; add rum and ice; top with soda.','Fresh, minty, citrusy, lightly sweet and sparkling.','A Cuban drink with antecedents in older rum-and-lime punches. The modern Mojito is associated with Havana; popular Hemingway claims are often overstated.'],
'Old Fashioned':['whiskey','spirit-forward','60 ml bourbon or rye; 7.5 ml rich syrup; 2 dashes aromatic bitters','Stir over ice; strain or build over a large cube. Express an orange peel.','Spirit-led, bittersweet, aromatic.','An early 19th-century family of spirit, sugar, water and bitters drinks. The “old-fashioned” label became popular as drinkers asked for cocktails made the old way; exact first bartender is uncertain.'],
'Negroni':['gin','bitter aperitivo','30 ml gin; 30 ml bitter aperitivo; 30 ml sweet vermouth','Build or stir over ice in a rocks glass; orange peel.','Bittersweet, herbal, citrus-oiled, full.','Widely associated with Florence in the early 20th century and Count Camillo Negroni, who reportedly asked for a stronger Americano-style drink. The anecdote is not definitive proof.']
